To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js when using WordPress as the backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install and configure the `cors` package in your Next.js project. You can do this by running the following command in your project directory:
```bash
npm install --save cors
```
Next, create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory and add the following code:
```javascript
// pages/api/cors.js
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with the URL of your WordPress site
});

export default async (req, res) => {
  await cors(req, res);
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the actual URL of your WordPress site. This will allow requests from your WordPress site to be made to your Next.js API routes.

2. In your Next.js API route files, use the `cors` middleware at the beginning of your route handler function. For example:
```javascript
// pages/api/example.js
import { cors } from '../../pages/api/cors';

export default async (req, res) => {
  await cors(req, res);
  // Your API route logic goes here
};
```
3. Finally, make sure to enable the `WP_REST_CORS_CONTROL` constant in your WordPress `wp-config.php` file to allow cross-origin requests from your Next.js app. Add the following line to your `wp-config.php` file:
```php
define('WP_REST_CORS_CONTROL', true);
```
Now, your Next.js app should be able to communicate with your WordPress site without any CORS errors.

