Sure! Here's a function that maps values from 0 to 1 to 0 to 1 in a smooth manner with a linear range in the middle:

```javascript
function smoothMap(x) {
  const smoothFactor = 0.2; // Adjust this value for the desired smoothness
  const linearRangeStart = 0.3; // Adjust this value for the start of the linear range
  const linearRangeEnd = 0.7; // Adjust this value for the end of the linear range

  if (x <= linearRangeStart) {
    return x * smoothFactor;
  } else if (x >= linearRangeEnd) {
    return (1 - x) * smoothFactor;
  } else {
    return x;
  }
}
```

This function takes a value `x` between 0 and 1, and returns a value between 0 and 1 that is smoothly mapped using a combination of a linear range and two smooth transitions. The `smoothFactor` variable controls the smoothness of the transitions, and the `linearRangeStart` and `linearRangeEnd` variables control the range of the linear section. You can adjust these values to customize the mapping behavior.

