To set up Auth0 SSO (Single Sign-On) for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, go to the "Applications" tab and click on your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID".
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` package by running `npm install @auth0/auth0-react`.
5. In each app's main file (e.g., `index.js`), import the `Auth0Provider` and pass in the `Domain` and `Client ID` from your Auth0 application:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

const auth0Provider = new Auth0Provider(
  'YOUR_DOMAIN',
  'YOUR_CLIENT_ID'
);

ReactDOM.render(
  <Auth0Provider {...auth0Provider}>
    ...your app content...
  </Auth0Provider>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```
6. In each app's login page or component, use the `useAuth0` hook to authenticate users:
```javascript
import { useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

function Login() {
  const { login } = useAuth0();

  const handleLogin = () => {
    login({
      connection: 'username-password-passwordless'
    });
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleLogin}>Login</button>
  );
}
```
7. Configure your Auth0 application to allow multiple applications to use the same credentials. To do this, go to the "Applications" tab in the Auth0 dashboard, click on your application, and go to the "Settings" tab. Under the "Allowed Applications" section, click on "Add Application" and enter the name and URL of each of your React apps.
8. Test your SSO setup by logging in to one of your React apps and verifying that you can access the other apps without having to re-enter your credentials.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *str = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char) * (argc + 1));
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(str, argv[i]);
        strcat(str, " ");
    }

    printf("Converted string: %s\n", str);
    free(str);

    return 0;
}
```

